{
  "requirement": "Every {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion` type implements\n`trust_but_verify::graph::Criterion<D>` for its\n{term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    type Ok: Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send;\n\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> D::NodeId;\n    fn criterion_path(&self) -> CriterionPath;\n    fn version(&self) -> CriterionVersion;\n    fn execute(\n        self,\n        ctx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>,\n    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject requires every criterion type to implement `Criterion<D>` for its application domain. The trait has four methods, `source_node_id`, `criterion_path`, `version` and an async `execute` that returns a `Send` future. The trait is `Send + Sized`, and `Ok` is `Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send`. This is mostly a compile-time contract, and the tests check it by implementing and using the trait.\n\n**Implementations.** Several test types implement the trait with exactly the required shape. Each one provides the `Ok` type, the three descriptor methods and an async `execute`. They are:\n- `Review<O>` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs`, for the `Application` domain. It is generic over `O: Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send`.\n- `Review` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs` and in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs`, for the `Memory` domain.\n- Other types for different domains, such as `StoredReview` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs` and `ParseAnswer` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs`.\n- The production `Review` for `ReviewGraph` in `src/authoring/sdk.rs`, which is exercised by the test `review_graph_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk`.\n\nAny extra required method, changed signature or changed associated-type bound in the trait would stop these implementations from compiling. They use different node-id types (`Node`, `u8`, `String`, `HabeasNode`), so the `D::NodeId` signatures are checked too.\n\n**Behavior through the trait.** The marked tests run these criteria through `Validator::verify`, `store_evidence` and `load_evidence`.\n- `criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested` and `caller_reuses_negative_answers_and_compares_revisions_explicitly` assert the values returned by `source_node_id`, `criterion_path` and `version`. They also check that `Ok` round-trips through serialization and deserialization.\n- `deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent` runs `execute` through the trait without an agent.\n\n**Bounds.** The `Send` bounds are checked by `validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs`.\n- It uses `Cell<u32>` as `Ok`, which is `Send` but not `Sync`.\n- Its `send` helper, `fn send<T: Send>`, wraps the futures returned by `verify`, `store_evidence`, `load_evidence` and `refresh_evidence`. That checks the futures built from `execute` are `Send`, and that `Ok` needs only `Send`, not `Sync`.\n\n**Delegated obligations.** Obligations specific to `answer`, `source_node_id` and `version` belong to the refining requirements and are not assessed here.\n\nI found no gap in the obligations stated by this requirement.",
  "references": "e1a7e692cdbf029708380bc9430c04a17aceac2c3ff46177628eca777f6f3080",
